RCP/M - SYSOP'S GUIDE

Read docs/INSTALLATION.DOC for first startup, client connections, listener
configuration and the optional tcpser/IP232 arrangement. This guide covers
routine administration. docs/DISK-IMAGES.DOC gives raw disk geometries and
format/edit commands.

SYSOP.DOC is a different, PRIVATE file generated locally at first start.
It contains your RBBS passwords. Do not publish it with these manuals.

Contents:
  1. The shared machine
  2. Disk and directory layout
  3. Local control, privileges and editing
  4. AUTH: caller permissions and executable approval
  5. RBBS administration
  6. Uploads, staging and review
  7. Archives and swappable volumes
  8. Shutdown, backup and recovery
  9. Updating this candidate

1. THE SHARED MACHINE

One cpmsim process runs CP/M 3, Z3PLUS and BYE510. The local terminal and
one connected caller share that guest; they are not independent sessions.
Keep the terminal open while serving calls. The distributed launcher has
no tmux dependency and does not create a background daemon.

RCPM.CONF selects direct TCP or IP232, address and port. Direct raw TCP on
127.0.0.1:4000 is the default. Changing settings takes effect at next start.
The listener reports successful startup only after it has opened the port.
If another listener owns that address/port, this guest cannot also bind it.

The current candidate uses a 4 MHz emulated CPU. CPU speed affects game
loops and software timing, as well as console throughput. Client display
pacing or an optional virtual modem is a separate way to reproduce a slow
connection. Do not treat a CPU clock change as merely a baud-rate setting.

2. DISK AND DIRECTORY LAYOUT

    A0:SYSTEM   Public commands, boot/support files and WELCOME.TXT
    A15:        Trusted RBBS executable and static board/display files
    I0:WORK     Temporary caller workspace
    I13:SYSUTLS  Private system utilities, including PIP, PPIP, SET and ZDE
    I14:HELP     Caller-readable help area; basic caller topics installed
    I15:RBBS     Private board users/messages/counters
    P0:SIGM     SIGMV001.LBR through SIGMV310.LBR
    P1:CPMUG    CPMUG001.LBR through CPMUG093.LBR
    J0:VOLUME   The currently mounted archive; caller read-only by default

I0:WORK is cleared before answering, including startup and call recovery.
Do not keep sysop documents, rescued edits or completed submissions there.
Other I: areas and the host upload inbox are not cleared by this routine.
There is currently no named CP/M UPLDS: area in the distributed directory
table. Incoming submissions are retained in the private host inbox instead.

The -NAME files are directory markers, not an automatic naming service.
The current named-directory table is configured separately. The marker
scanner remains future work. NAME without a new name can delete a table
entry; do not use a bare NAME command just to inspect directories. PWD and
ZSHOW provide directory-table inspection.

Caller HELP topics live in I14:. HELP opens HELP.HLP by default; HELP GAMES
opens GAMES.HLP. DIR HELP: lists the topic filenames. Fresh builds include
commands, mounting, library browsing, transfers, RBBS, games, WORK and logoff.
These are plain-text topics for the preserved HELPC viewer, not Markdown.
Use a semicolon on the first line, CP/M CR/LF text and a Ctrl-Z end marker.
Keep the supplied topics short enough for one screen. Maintain them privately
with the caller blanked; caller read access does not permit editing HELP.

3. LOCAL CONTROL, PRIVILEGES AND EDITING

With a caller connected, local Ctrl-B blanks the caller and enables private
sysop operation. Ctrl-B again restores shared operation and restrictions.
Private output stays local. Return to A0:SYSTEM or another public directory
before unblanking. Selecting a drive/user area does not grant file access;
the shared permission checks apply to file operations in caller mode.

While private, local control keys reach the application except Ctrl-B.
In shared mode, historic BYE controls remain: Ctrl-Q enters 'Message from
Sysop', Ctrl-X leaves that message mode, and Ctrl-N forces caller hangup.
These are BYE controls, not editor commands. Leave private editors before
unblanking. A private directory must not become caller-readable merely
because the sysop selected it.

Live chat: a caller types CHAT at the CP/M prompt (leave RBBS with C first).
Answer with Escape on the local console. Both terminals need 80x24 ANSI/VT100:
SYSOP uses the upper pane; CALLER uses the lower pane. Each pane wraps and
scrolls independently. Backspace erases on the current line; Tab adds spaces.
Ctrl-R redraws both panes; Ctrl-C or Ctrl-X from either keyboard ends chat.
CHAT /S retains the plain scrolling display for simpler terminals.
Local Ctrl-B ends split chat and enters private-console mode. Subsequent
private input is not stored in chat buffers. Ctrl-G toggles BYE's page bell
and paging availability. An unanswered page returns to CP/M. HELP CHAT gives
caller instructions. See CHAT46.md for build details and validation.

If using your own tmux wrapper, Ctrl-B Ctrl-B passes one Ctrl-B through tmux.
The distributed launcher itself uses no tmux and takes a single Ctrl-B.

Use SYSUTLS:ZDE A0:WELCOME.TXT to edit the caller sign-on banner privately.
ZDE uses Ctrl-K then S to save, Ctrl-K then X to save/exit, and Ctrl-K then Q
to abandon changes. If an unchanged buffer prompts to save after an earlier
save, answer N to exit without repeating the save. WELCOME.TXT is selected
exactly; an editor's WELCOME.BAK should not replace it as the sign-on file.

Public commands are deliberately reviewed and limited. AUTH controls caller
read/write permissions by drive and user area. Caller COM execution also
requires F4 approval and a caller-read-only source DU; see section 4. Copying
a COM file to writable WORK does not make it executable. SYS affects directory
display, not execution authority. The fixed A0:RBBS launcher has a narrow
exception for its trusted private A15: board and I15: data, without granting
wheel privileges or general caller access to those directories.

4. AUTH: CALLER PERMISSIONS AND EXECUTABLE APPROVAL

AUTH.COM resides in I13:SYSUTLS. It administers the running BYE permission
table and individual COM-file approvals. You do not need to stop the server,
rebuild BYE, or remount an image after using AUTH inside CP/M.

4.1 Starting AUTH safely

With a caller connected, use local Ctrl-B to blank the caller and enter
private sysop mode. Then use SYSUTLS:AUTH, or simply AUTH if the private
command path is active. Keep the caller blanked throughout administration.
Return to A0:SYSTEM before pressing Ctrl-B again to restore caller operation.

If administering between calls, local Ctrl-C opens BYE's menu. Select J to
jump to CP/M with BYE attached. AUTH needs that resident BYE interface;
Return for normal unpatched CP/M removes it and is not the AUTH environment.

AUTH rejects administration outside private sysop mode, even if someone
copies AUTH.COM to a public DU and marks it executable. These permissions
control the caller, not the private operator. Private mode permits maintenance
on A:, I:, J: and P: through the same policy bypass. Genuine host read-only
media, ordinary CP/M file R/O attributes and other file errors still apply.

Display help with:

    SYSUTLS:AUTH //

Commands and filenames are case-insensitive. AUTH accepts one target per
invocation; use several commands in a SUBMIT file or alias for a sequence.

4.2 The permission model and shipped defaults

D: means a drive, for example J:. DU: means a drive plus user area, for example
J3:. Each drive has a caller permission pair. Each DU either inherits that
pair or has an explicit replacement. The order is:

    explicit DU setting -> drive setting -> default NONE

An explicit DU setting always takes precedence, including an explicit NONE.
A drive change preserves existing DU overrides. There are no automatic private
exceptions for particular drive letters: SYSUTLS and RBBS data are private
because of the configured policy, with a separate controlled RBBS service grant.

The supplied startup policy grants:

    Scope                     Caller permission
    A0:SYSTEM                 R-  (public commands and banner)
    J: and P:                 R-  (public collections)
    I0:WORK                   RW  (temporary workspace)
    I14:HELP                  R-  (help topics)
    Other DUs                 --  (neither read nor write)

I13:SYSUTLS and I15:RBBS inherit I:'s NONE setting in that policy. If you grant
access to all of I:, those inherited DUs follow it too. To keep particular DUs
private under a broader drive grant, give them explicit NONE overrides first.
Keep the policy files and private board data protected against caller writes.

R permits file reading and directory browsing. W permits file mutations
through available programs. Write-only settings deny reading/listing; utilities
that require read access for file lookup may not work with a write-only DU.
RW is permission, not a supplied file-transfer or copy service: callers still
have no general copy command or direct upload into WORK in this package.
The game launchers seed WORK, and caller programs can create workspace data.
UPLOAD continues to use the private host inbox described in section 6.

I0:WORK is cleared at startup and call recovery regardless of its AUTH setting.
Changing its permissions does not turn it into permanent storage.

4.3 Reading the map and querying targets

    AUTH                      All drives, users 0-15
    AUTH /MAP                 Same complete map
    AUTH J:                   Only the J: row, users 0-15
    AUTH J3:                  Same complete J: row

These commands only display information. AUTH D: does NOT reset D: to NONE.
No permission change occurs without an explicit permission argument.

The map shows effective caller permissions for each DU. An asterisk before
the pair means that DU has an explicit override:

    *RW   Explicit read/write       *R-   Explicit read-only
    *-W   Explicit write-only       *--   Explicit denial
     R-   Read-only inherited from the drive (no asterisk)

A missing asterisk means inheritance, even when the inherited value is --.
The map does not show whether a disk is mounted or whether a particular file
exists. You can configure G3: even if no G: image is currently attached.

4.4 Setting drive and DU permissions

Both slash syntax and map-style pairs are supported:

    Slash form       Map form       Also accepted       Result
    /RW              RW             RW                  Read and write
    /R               R-             R                   Read only
    /W               -W             W                   Write only
    /NONE            --             NONE                Neither

Every form REPLACES the complete pair. /W does not add W to an existing R;
it sets -W and removes read access. Likewise, /R removes write access.
Do not type the map's asterisk as part of a permission argument.

    AUTH J: /R                Set the drive's caller pair to R-
    AUTH J3: RW               Override J3: with read/write
    AUTH J3: /W               Replace that override with write-only
    AUTH J3: --               Explicitly deny both permissions on J3:
    AUTH J3: INHERIT           Remove its override; follow J: again

/INHERIT is equivalent to INHERIT and applies only to a DU. With J: set to R-,
J3: INHERIT becomes R- and loses its asterisk. Explicit J3: NONE remains denied
and keeps its asterisk even if the drive is readable. To deny a drive's base
permissions, use AUTH J: /NONE; that does not erase its explicit DU overrides.

Changes take effect in BYE's resident map immediately and survive caller
hangups. They remain unsaved until you use /SAVE. They are replaced by /LOAD
or by the next startup's saved map.

4.5 COM-file execution and F4

Normal caller execution requires ALL of the following:

    - Read access to the executable's DU.
    - No caller write access to that DU.
    - F4 set on the actual COM file's directory entry.

The source DU matters, not the caller's current working directory. A caller
in writable I0: can run approved commands found on read-only A0:. The caller
cannot run a COM stored in writable I0:, even if it has F4 set. If you make
I0: caller-read-only, approved files there can run; they are still cleared
at call boundaries. Individual file R/O does not override the writable-DU ban.

All ordinary COM files use this rule, including public commands in A0:.
Setting A0: to RW therefore stops callers loading DIR, SDZ, LT and the other
COM commands there. Private sysop operation continues to work. To restore
normal caller commands, set AUTH A0: /R. See troubleshooting below.

Inspect, approve or revoke an existing exact filename:

    AUTH J1:ADV.COM            Report effective caller executable status
    AUTH J1:ADV.COM +X         Set F4; preserve other file attributes
    AUTH J1:ADV.COM -X         Clear F4; preserve other file attributes

The target comes first. AUTH +X J1:ADV.COM is not supported. These file commands
require a DU prefix, a COM extension and an exact name; wildcards are rejected.

Examples of status output:

    J1:ADV.COM is executable.
    J1:ADV.COM is not executable: F4 is not set.
    J1:ADV.COM is not executable: DU is caller-writable.
    J1:ADV.COM is not executable: no caller read access.
    J1:ADV.COM not found.

+X is approval, not a way to override DU permissions. F4 is our caller-approval
convention, not a universal meaning imposed by CP/M. Clear inherited F4 flags
when importing software, then approve reviewed programs deliberately. Individual
file R/O is optional for execution and retains its normal CP/M meaning. SET
can also manage F4 in private mode. SYS controls directory display, not
approval.

F4 changes are written directly to the file directory. They do NOT require
AUTH /SAVE. Conversely, AUTH /LOAD does not undo any F4 changes. If a copied
file retains F4, it still cannot execute while its DU is caller-writable.

Approval means trusting the program, including external loaders, interpreters,
shell escapes and direct memory/disk operations it may provide. BYE is not a
sandbox for hostile Z80 code. The reviewed RBBS entry has its own narrow grant
for the private board executable/data; do not make those DUs public to run it.

4.6 Live settings, saved files and startup

BYE reads I13:AUTH.DAT into memory when its resident policy is initialized.
File checks use that memory table. AUTH's drive/DU commands update only that
table; they do not silently rewrite the startup file.

    /SAVE    Write the live drive/DU map to a chosen policy file.
    /LOAD    Validate a chosen policy file and replace the live map.

Saved files include drive settings and DU overrides. They do not contain
COM-file F4 approvals, accounts, passwords or disk images. Startup always uses
I13:AUTH.DAT; loading an alternate file does not change that startup filename.
Each interactive prompt still defaults to I13:AUTH.DAT, not the last-used file.
SAVE/LOAD always handle the entire map, not only the last drive you queried.
Policy files are binary; manage them with AUTH rather than a text editor.
Changing a saved file alone does not change the resident map until LOAD or
startup.

Bare forms prompt:

    AUTH /SAVE
    Save policy to [I13:AUTH.DAT]:

    AUTH /LOAD
    Load policy from [I13:AUTH.DAT]:

Press Return to select I13:AUTH.DAT, or enter an alternate filename. Saving
interactively asks before overwriting an existing file; answer Y to proceed
or N/Return to cancel. Cancelling leaves the destination unchanged.

Unqualified filenames use I13:, even if you started AUTH in another directory.
A drive-only prefix uses the current user area when AUTH starts; an explicit
DU selects that area. Scripts should use explicit DU filenames for clarity.
Names use exact CP/M 8.3 format and do not accept wildcards. These are CP/M
disk filenames, not host operating-system paths.

4.7 Scripts, explicit filenames and overwrite authorization

    AUTH /SAVE=I13:PUBLIC.DAT
    AUTH /SAVE=I13:PUBLIC.DAT /Y
    AUTH /LOAD=I13:PUBLIC.DAT

With =filename these commands run without filename or overwrite prompts.
/SAVE=filename creates a new destination, but if it already exists the command
fails with "File exists; use /Y to overwrite." It does not wait for input.
Adding /Y authorizes overwrite and rotation of the prior same-basename backup.
/Y does not bypass private-mode checks, read-only media or file errors.
It is a SAVE option, not a LOAD option.

For normal startup-policy maintenance, a complete scripted save is:

    AUTH /SAVE=I13:AUTH.DAT /Y

AUTH reports success/failure and sets CP/M 3's program return code: 0000h on
success, FF01h on error or cancellation. How a batch file reacts depends on
its command processor/automation tools. A status query reporting that an
existing COM is not executable is a successful query; a missing file is an
error. Run scripts that administer policy only in private sysop mode.

AUTH flushes pending CP/M disk buffers before changing live DU permissions,
including /LOAD. If flushing fails, the live permissions remain unchanged.
There is no added delay or restriction on sysop permission choices.

4.8 Worked policy workflows

Restore the normal public system directory and temporary workspace:

    AUTH A0: /R
    AUTH I0: RW
    AUTH /SAVE=I13:AUTH.DAT /Y

Try a temporary change without making it the startup policy:

    AUTH G3: RW
    AUTH G3:
    AUTH /LOAD=I13:AUTH.DAT

The load restores the saved map, discarding unsaved DU changes such as G3:.
It does not reset per-file F4 flags.

Keep an alternate policy and select it later:

    AUTH /SAVE=I13:PUBLIC.DAT
    AUTH J3: --
    AUTH /SAVE=I13:LIMITED.DAT
    AUTH /LOAD=I13:PUBLIC.DAT
    AUTH /LOAD=I13:LIMITED.DAT

The first two saves above assume unused filenames. Add /Y if deliberately
overwriting an existing version. Loading LIMITED.DAT changes the running map
only. To use that live map after restart as well:

    AUTH /SAVE=I13:AUTH.DAT /Y

Approve Adventure, with GAMES already selected on J::

    AUTH J1:ADV.COM
    AUTH J1:ADV.COM +X
    AUTH J1:ADV.COM

No remount or BYE rebuild is required. Restoring caller mode applies the normal
DU/F4 checks. A writable game-data workspace does not require making J: writable
to the caller. Games that insist on writes to J: remain download/local-play
software, not online games we must accommodate.

4.9 Backups, failed saves and recovery

For a destination such as PUBLIC.DAT, SAVE first writes a complete PUBLIC.$$$
in the same DU. It closes and flushes that file, renames an existing destination
to PUBLIC.BAK (replacing the older backup), then installs the new PUBLIC.DAT.
The ordinary AUTH.DAT backup is AUTH.BAK. This backup is a policy-file backup,
not a backup of the board or its disk images.

.BAK and .$$$ are reserved SAVE extensions. LOAD can read such files if their
contents are a valid policy. An existing temporary file is not silently erased,
even with /Y. After an I/O error retain all temporary/backup files for
inspection.
If installing the new destination fails, AUTH attempts to rename the backup
back into place. Failure of that recovery or a power loss can still leave a
missing startup file; keep whole-installation backups as described in section 8.

LOAD validates the signature, complete required records and every permission
byte before changing the resident table. An invalid, incomplete or missing file
leaves the current live map unchanged. If BYE cannot load a valid startup
policy, caller file access stays denied and a local diagnostic is displayed.
BYE does not automatically select AUTH.BAK or another alternate policy.

Recover a valid backup through private AUTH, without restarting:

    AUTH /LOAD=I13:AUTH.BAK
    AUTH /SAVE=I13:RESCUE.DAT
    AUTH /SAVE=I13:AUTH.DAT /Y

Use an unused name for RESCUE.DAT. Preserve that good copy before overwriting
AUTH.DAT: the final save rotates the old (possibly corrupt) AUTH.DAT into
AUTH.BAK, replacing the backup you just loaded. If a leftover AUTH.$$$ prevents
saving, first secure a valid policy under a different basename, then inspect
and clear only the stale staging file using private maintenance tools.

If no valid policy file remains, use private AUTH to rebuild the desired drive
and DU grants, inspect /MAP, then save them as I13:AUTH.DAT. The defaults table
in section 4.2 is a reference, not an automatic reset command.

4.10 Troubleshooting

DIR?, SDZ? or LT? after making A0: RW:
    Caller-writable DUs cannot supply caller executables. Blank the caller and
    run AUTH A0: /R. Save the corrected map if the bad setting was saved.
    Query AUTH A0:DIR.COM if it still fails; also check that the file exists.

Game preparation failed:
    MOUNT GAMES first. I0: needs read/write access for the supplied launchers.
    Use AUTH I0: RW in private mode if necessary. The game executable's DU
    still needs caller read access, no write access and F4 approval.

I0: changed to -- after /INHERIT:
    It now follows I:'s NONE setting. Restore the explicit AUTH I0: RW override.

/LOAD did not undo an executable approval:
    Policy files hold DU permissions only. Use AUTH DU:FILE.COM -X to clear F4.

Changes disappeared after restart:
    Save to I13:AUTH.DAT, not just an alternate filename. Unsaved changes
    survive call boundaries but not a new BYE startup. Check that the save
    succeeded.

AUTH reports that private mode or an AUTH-enabled driver is required:
    Blank the caller locally. With no caller, use BYE's J menu choice to keep
    its resident interface attached. Do not use normal unpatched CP/M for AUTH.
    Check that the driver and utility came from a compatible package.

Explicit /SAVE says the file exists:
    Use /Y only if you intend to overwrite it, or choose a new filename.

Save reports a staging file already exists:
    Inspect the same-basename .$$$ before removing it. It may contain a complete
    recoverable map from a previous interrupted save; see section 4.9.

The optional host --caller-allow/deny/list helper is for stopped-image
maintenance. Normal live administration uses AUTH inside CP/M.

5. RBBS 4.1 ADMINISTRATION

Fresh packages contain empty PERSONAL, GENERAL, CPM and TEST areas and only
one SYSOP seed account. First start generates a unique six-character password
in private SYSOP.DOC. Enter SYSOP at "Enter your name", then that password.
Passwords are case-sensitive. The displayed 123456 is a character counter.
New callers can register and post without validation.

Normal menus act on a single key. Text and message numbers still use Return.
Expert mode retains line commands and semicolon sequences (R;1, R;1+).
End message text with a period on its own line, then S to save.
F selects a forum; R reads; S summarizes; K deletes an authorized message.
N reads new messages. C returns to CP/M; G disconnects.

Use the local console for maintenance. Ctrl-B blanks a connected caller and
permits private operation. Run SYSUTLS:RBUTIL41, then use its numbered menu:
4 selects a forum; 5 adds a forum; 6 manages users; 9 quits.
Option 3 RESETS THE ACCOUNT DATABASE. It is not routine maintenance.
Forum names have at most eight characters; capacity is eight areas total,
including PERSONAL. Back up before adding areas or changing user privileges.
Restore caller access with Ctrl-B after maintenance.

The protected program is A15:RBBS41.COM. The users, callers, subject list,
message files and display files are on I15:. SYSUTLS:RBUTIL41 is private.
Stop the system before host-side editing. Back up the entire installation,
including SYSOP.DOC, state/ and disks/. Do not remove state/initialized to
reset credentials. SYSOP.DOC records the original generated password.

RBBS 4.1 uses different data files from 3.7. Fresh release disks are not an
in-place migration and must not replace a running board's account/message
files. Keep existing installations and backups separately.

6. UPLOADS, STAGING AND REVIEW

UPLOAD filename.ext receives XMODEM/CRC; UPLOAD /Y receives a YMODEM batch.
Completed files are saved OUTSIDE the CP/M disks in the host directory:

    uploads/inbox/

In-progress files use:

    uploads/staging/

These directories sit beside disks/, runtime/ and RCPM.CONF in the unpacked
installation. They are private operator storage, not a mounted CP/M drive.
The caller cannot browse them through DIR, and uploading does not create an
executable file in WORK or another CP/M user area. No UPLDS: alias is currently
provided for this host inbox.

The service uses unique filename suffixes and does not overwrite an earlier
submission of the same name. Completed files are flushed and committed before
success is reported. Interrupted partial files are discarded from staging;
a completed file from an earlier part of a YMODEM batch remains even if a
later file is interrupted. XMODEM retains record padding; YMODEM retains the
declared file length. Leave staging and inbox on the same host filesystem,
because committing a submission uses an atomic hard-link operation.

MESSAGE submissions are also saved in uploads/inbox/ when the caller saves
the note. Unsaved drafts are discarded at call recovery. Completed uploads
and saved messages survive disconnect, WORK cleanup and normal restart.

Review procedure:

  1. Inspect the completed inbox file and its accompanying documentation.
  2. Check the real content, archive members, naming and distribution notices.
     Do not infer trust solely from a filename or the upload protocol.
  3. Keep a copy of the original submission. Decide where an accepted file
     belongs; completed uploads are not automatically public publications.
  4. Stop RCP/M and back up the target image before host-side disk editing.
  5. Import reviewed files into a suitable archive image using the bundled
     cpmtools and exact geometry described in docs/DISK-IMAGES.DOC.
  6. Retain owner-writable image permissions for private sysop maintenance,
     restart, and verify caller access with AUTH and the intended contents.

Do not publish by placing an arbitrary file in disks/library/: that catalog contains
complete, correctly formatted J: disk images. To build a new public volume,
prepare it outside the catalog, inspect it, then add the finished image under
an uppercase 1-8 character name such as GAMES.dsk. Never host-edit a mounted
image while cpmsim is running.

7. ARCHIVES AND SWAPPABLE VOLUMES

MOUNT /L lists both J: and P: catalogs and their current images.
MOUNT NAME finds a name in either catalog, then uses its exact size to select
J: (4177920 bytes) or P: (536870912 bytes). For example, MOUNT WALNUT
reports: Volume WALNUT mounted on P:
MOUNT J: /L and MOUNT P: /L list only the selected catalog.
If both catalogs contain the same name, use MOUNT J: NAME or MOUNT P: NAME;
an unqualified ambiguous name leaves both drives unchanged.
MOUNT J: /DEFAULT and MOUNT P: /DEFAULT restore the startup images.
J: requires 4177920-byte images; P: requires 536870912-byte images. Wrong
sizes are rejected before replacing the mounted image. AUTH rules are the same
on both drives; grants apply to drive/user slots, not image names.

After a successful swap and relog, MOUNT chains to A0:NDSCAN drive: /Q. NDSCAN
can also run directly. It rebuilds only that drive's names from extensionless
-NAME files in user areas 0-15, clearing names where no marker exists. Other
drives and AUTH settings are unchanged. Use 1-7 uppercase letters/digits or
underscores after the hyphen; -CATALOG.011 is not a marker. Multiple markers,
name collisions, disk errors or a full name table abort the name update.
The prepared Z3PLUS table holds 42 names (768 bytes).

MOUNT suppresses the scanner success message; scan errors are still displayed.
Callers may run NDSCAN on fully readable drives; private sysops may scan private
drives. A denied user area aborts scanning rather than clearing unseen names.
If NDSCAN fails after MOUNT, the new media remains mounted but names stay as
before: correct the problem and rerun NDSCAN or restore /DEFAULT. Use explicit
drive/user addresses while names are stale. SYSOP permissions remain unchanged.
Before replacing the mounted image, MOUNT flushes pending CP/M disk buffers.
If flushing fails, it leaves the existing image mounted. Catalog listing alone
does not flush or change media. After a successful swap, MOUNT resets/relogs
the selected drive.
DIR J: lists files; LT J:README.TXT reads a volume guide. LDIR lists LBR members
and LT reads text members. YSEND downloads through guest BDOS using YMODEM.

GAMES contains playable games, required data and playing instructions:
  J0: guide and preparation launchers; J1: expanded Adventure;
  J8: Animals; J3: Maze Chase. The optional expanded edition adds 73 titles.
Source-only and unqualified sets formerly on J4-J7, source files, old catalogs
and MCHORIG.COM are excluded. Original archives remain separately on P:.
After MOUNT GAMES, type J0:ADVENT, J0:ANIMALS or J0:MCHASE. Launchers copy
needed data into WORK and run the approved game on J:. Maze Chase uses
ANSI/VT100. WORK saves and high scores last only until logoff.
J0:ADVENT, ANIMALS and MCHASE are our preparation launchers, rather than the
original game executables. No game-specific wrappers occupy A0:.
ADVENT starts the unchanged Adventure program at
J1:ADV.COM after preparing its data in WORK. Games that require writes to the
protected J: collection are for download and local play. We do not promise
online compatibility for every game in a collection.
GUIDES contains documentation only. There is no public development-tool volume.
Private tools belong in I13:SYSUTLS or offline media.

AUTH permissions on J: apply to the mounted drive/user slots, not to a catalog
image name. Replacing GAMES with another image does not reset those permissions.
Under the default J: read grant, every mounted image is caller-readable unless
a DU override denies access. Keeping an image under disks/library/offline/ prevents caller
MOUNT from listing it; it does not make its contents private if you mount it
in a caller-readable J: slot. Restore a suitable image before unblanking.

P: holds the permanent SIGM and CPMUG libraries. metadata/ARCHIVE.sha256 records all
403 file hashes; metadata/VOLUMES.json records the curated J: image contents. Preserve
original library/member notices when rebuilding or adding to collections.

8. SHUTDOWN, BACKUP AND RECOVERY

BYE ends the caller's connection and leaves the server waiting.
To stop between calls, use the local console: Ctrl-C opens BYE's menu;
Return enters normal local CP/M. Wait for the prompt, then enter
SYSUTLS:BYEBYE. If already at a private CP/M prompt, the same command stops
the emulator. Let the shutdown finish before copying images or restarting.

While stopped, copy the whole installation directory to separate storage.
Include disks/, disks/library/, uploads/inbox/, state/, RCPM.CONF and private
SYSOP.DOC. The emulator must remain stopped during the snapshot. An idle
caller connection or a blanked caller is not the same as a stopped emulator.

Restore into a NEW directory from one complete snapshot, not by mixing
database pieces from different dates. Stop any original instance using the
same listener port before starting the restored copy. Do not unpack a fresh
release over an installation that contains your active board and credentials.
A fresh unpack is a new board with new first-start passwords.

The launcher holds .console.lock through cpmsim's lifetime to refuse another
process on the same installation. Do not delete lock files or bypass the
launcher to open those disks concurrently. For public-service backups, retain
an additional offline copy; another folder on the same disk does not protect
against loss of that disk.

If WORK cleanup reports failure, answering remains disabled. Stop and repair
the I: image or its host write permissions before trying again. Read-only
system attributes inside CP/M and read-only host filesystem permissions are
different mechanisms; consult docs/DISK-IMAGES.DOC when diagnosing either one.

9. UPDATING THIS CANDIDATE

Keep your existing installation and its backup. Test a new release in a
separate directory first. Full migration of a live board is an operator task;
do not replace I: with a fresh image if you intend to retain its accounts.
Install matching emulator and BYE driver versions together. Select MODE in
RCPM.CONF and restart to switch direct/IP232; do not change mounted drivers.

Read docs/DISTRIBUTION-STATUS.DOC before redistributing this aggregate. Original
Z3PLUS, BYE5, utility and archive notices have not been replaced with a uniform
license. This local candidate has not been published as release 1.0.

The package's disks/library/offline/BUILD.dsk retains the DRI development files. Microsoft
M80/L80 and MicroPro WM (including WM.HLP) are separately supplied build inputs and are omitted
from that image and from the source bundle's original toolchain-disk copies.
They are not required to run the system. metadata/DEVELOPMENT-MEDIA.json records the
separation; source/BUILDING.DOC explains how builders supply their own inputs.
